#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133612 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d177b305-cdd1-5f91-b9d5-90623e52ad10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given a QR Factorization"}] :question/id #uuid "5fb2571c-2ab9-5fe5-9454-f9d7b0e89854" :question/math-academy-id "e-5592" :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where\n![](images/e0/e0a49a8ee02f2bcc10900dcaaa30969d4ab610dd703135ad4418c78272ebbca0.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, it satisfies $Q^T Q = I,$ and we get\n\n$$\nR^T R \\mathbf{x} = R^T \\! Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R^T$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $(R^T)^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}28 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=28 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=7.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}-3(7) & =28 \\\\ 7{x}_{1} & =49 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is ![](images/c3/c3f08e2cdc175b34030857c03d0301e5b734975355d09b2016559b4f28b6f5e4.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133719 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133906 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7d226d3-f7d3-574f-94f9-a532ec99b0ac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Expression for a Least-Squares Solution in Terms of the Matrices Q and R"}] :question/id #uuid "d805b370-4377-5101-8ac7-3d30e11156ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-9917" :question/problem "$$\n\\bbox[5px, border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}} \\, \\mathbf{x} = \\bbox[5px, border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}} \\, \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the matrix equation above gives the least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\nA^T A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, it satisfies $Q^T Q = I,$ and we get\n\n$$\nR^T R \\mathbf{x} = R^T \\! Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R^T$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $(R^T)^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ \\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\,R\\phantom{|}}\\,\\,x & ={Q}^{T}\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are $R$ and $Q^T.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133906 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7d226d3-f7d3-574f-94f9-a532ec99b0ac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Expression for a Least-Squares Solution in Terms of the Matrices Q and R"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29c6894b-ca73-55e4-b9e5-4515a24ac505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $b$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440834} {:answer/id #uuid "2b99f2e8-4b05-5e94-b564-250f2853615a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m\\cdot {Q}^{-1}$ and $b$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440835} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd231d7-89bb-5f54-b089-0db260de5eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$b$ and $Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440836} {:answer/id #uuid "eef0bb49-3a2a-51f2-9a63-905441460146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Q}^{T}$ and $b$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440837} {:answer/id #uuid "efe2b6b7-9514-56b9-905d-32ae3b168ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $b$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eef0bb49-3a2a-51f2-9a63-905441460146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Q}^{T}$ and $b$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440837} :answer-field/id #uuid "75513403-98f8-5580-8da1-119fb356a265" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5d70a3f-a448-5de5-8896-cf994f65054e" :question/math-academy-id "q-139751" :question/problem "$$\nx={R}^{-1}\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nThen, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by ${R}^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b \\\\ x & ={R}^{-1}\\,\\,\\bbox[6px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{|}{Q}^{T}\\,\\,}\\,\\,\\bbox[6px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{|}b\\phantom{|}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are ${Q}^{T}$ and $b.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133906 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7d226d3-f7d3-574f-94f9-a532ec99b0ac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Expression for a Least-Squares Solution in Terms of the Matrices Q and R"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c8b0ca7-13e5-55bd-8b2e-cfb0f049d7dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433678} {:answer/id #uuid "48addc78-f040-5625-b69b-edcbf42f838e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433679} {:answer/id #uuid "5101d8c8-7e8d-5948-84c1-8bbb8a5a3fa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433680} {:answer/id #uuid "75c877de-c07f-583c-9fdd-ddb1c7b23449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433681} {:answer/id #uuid "8091e143-1c62-5487-9984-834771a83882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(R-{A}^{T}A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c8b0ca7-13e5-55bd-8b2e-cfb0f049d7dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433678} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bf75937-c3db-5f40-8e20-ee3efbdf6a43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "553d1e63-f638-55c5-a882-d8b6a7339c64" :question/math-academy-id "q-139766" :question/problem "$$\n\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,x={Q}^{T}\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the matrix equation above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ What is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ R\\,\\,x & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing part of the equation is $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d177b305-cdd1-5f91-b9d5-90623e52ad10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb9d78f4-aa3e-5f6c-9e00-cb88d8c0a1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363170} {:answer/id #uuid "61095090-5603-5eb4-acb6-44d1a726d375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363171} {:answer/id #uuid "54e79a64-d102-5ec7-92fb-704283cbd246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363172} {:answer/id #uuid "d091dad6-c30c-5c5a-93cb-462966115e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363173} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5d8ca3-4cb5-5f45-9f6b-8f05c6c59c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb9d78f4-aa3e-5f6c-9e00-cb88d8c0a1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363170} :answer-field/id #uuid "67e03da2-3c94-5901-94b3-848cec26cc95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1989d3b4-7a5e-5b18-8a4f-6c042b9559dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-139795" :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,R=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} & 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}15\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}=15\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2}=6\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-3\\sqrt{2}(3) & =15\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1} & =24\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d177b305-cdd1-5f91-b9d5-90623e52ad10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14352d53-a485-5307-b112-104879c985d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388955} {:answer/id #uuid "05dada7d-111e-5f20-88ac-6afa8f9f9c8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388956} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc051c8-5415-519f-a519-b6e946874fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388957} {:answer/id #uuid "614b492c-6971-59f9-aed8-3ee263cec78b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388958} {:answer/id #uuid "f2b1478f-eae1-527f-ba01-427d7b9d60b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dc051c8-5415-519f-a519-b6e946874fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388957} :answer-field/id #uuid "94da062c-31ea-5fc0-b57d-fe4122f66c74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc07a546-4716-553a-9b4a-217a6ed81854" :question/math-academy-id "q-157051" :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 24 \\\\ -24 & -7\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is orthogonal, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -24 \\\\ 24 & -7\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=-11 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-(2) & =-11 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133906 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7d226d3-f7d3-574f-94f9-a532ec99b0ac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Expression for a Least-Squares Solution in Terms of the Matrices Q and R"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f67bd72e-fdc1-5a03-8447-bfd09a70f376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401092} {:answer/id #uuid "01a67471-d12d-5153-bc2d-d06208bd68e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401093} {:answer/id #uuid "c4dbeaf3-ad0e-5cbe-a0fc-7d2689833b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401094} {:answer/id #uuid "66035904-de9d-5004-9133-f65b6dd564b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and ${Q}^{T}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401095} {:answer/id #uuid "f8434c0c-cb29-5697-82e4-05952a54d5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and ${R}^{T}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66035904-de9d-5004-9133-f65b6dd564b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and ${Q}^{T}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401095} :answer-field/id #uuid "474be29c-51a5-5850-8515-5dee1fd67caa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f81ecce3-b49e-5261-aee2-21f0b16a1f87" :question/math-academy-id "q-157052" :question/problem "$$\n\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,x=\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the matrix equation above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ R\\,\\,x & ={Q}^{T}\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are $R$ and ${Q}^{T}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133906 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7d226d3-f7d3-574f-94f9-a532ec99b0ac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Expression for a Least-Squares Solution in Terms of the Matrices Q and R"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fdcd9d7-d7a2-5fbc-b814-a5cdbab0aba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}^{-1}$ and ${Q}^{T}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401099} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac28ba0-e324-56db-ad83-179ae52ae559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}^{-1}$ and $Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401100} {:answer/id #uuid "7921d298-b867-528e-8983-2fecc690fc56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401101} {:answer/id #uuid "b00b89ef-c0e1-594a-b11a-06139f3f787e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and ${Q}^{T}$" :db/id #edb/ref 1759